## 4. Conventions and prior work the study should follow or compare against

**Intervals for repeated k-fold CV (S4, S5).** The 50 outer fold scores are not independent (training sets overlap),
so a plain t-interval over 50 folds is too narrow. The standard correction is the corrected resampled t
(Nadeau & Bengio 2003, "Inference for the generalization error", Machine Learning 52:239-281,
doi:10.1023/A:1024068626366), applied to repeated k-fold by Bouckaert & Frank (2004, PAKDD, LNCS 3056:3-12,
doi:10.1007/978-3-540-24775-3_3): with J = 50 fold scores of mean m and sample variance s^2,
`m ± t_{0.975, J-1} * sqrt((1/J + n_test/n_train) * s^2)`, here n_test/n_train = 30/120 = 0.25.
Recommendation from context: use this for both per-method means and the paired difference (difference computed
fold by fold on identical splits), and say so in the paper. Which interval is used is the analysis owner's decision
(`experiment-repo`); whatever is chosen must be named and justified in the paper. A naive interval may be shown only
alongside, labelled as such. Dietterich (1998, Neural Computation 10(7):1895-1923, doi:10.1162/089976698300017197)
is the classic reference on paired tests for comparing classifiers.

**Nested CV for k (S2).** Inner stratified CV on each outer training set (e.g. 5-fold), grid of k (e.g. 1..30 or odd
1..29), selection by inner mean accuracy with a stated tie rule (scikit-learn `GridSearchCV` keeps the first best,
i.e. smallest k). Any preprocessing (e.g. standardization, which matters for kNN distances) must be fitted inside
the training fold (pipeline). The distribution of selected k over the 50 outer folds is a natural by-product to report.

**Logistic regression (S1).** scikit-learn `LogisticRegression` with `lbfgs` fits a multinomial (softmax) model for
multiclass targets; the `multi_class` argument is deprecated in recent versions. The objective does not ask to tune
its regularization; a fixed, stated setting (default L2, C=1, with or without standardization, stated) is faithful.
Tuning C would be an addition, not a requirement.

**Expected magnitudes (planning estimates, not results).** Both methods are widely reported at roughly 95-97% CV
accuracy on Iris; the difference is expected to be within about one percentage point and its interval to include 0.
Each outer test fold has 30 samples, so fold accuracies move in steps of 1/30 (3.3 points). A "no detectable
difference" result is a legitimate finding and should be reported as such.

**Reproducibility (S6-S8).** Fix all seeds (outer `RepeatedStratifiedKFold` random_state, inner splits), pin
package versions identically on Ibex and locally, and save per-fold results from both runs; compare per-fold
accuracies and selected k exactly (bitwise), not only the summaries. Record the node name from the job itself
(`$SLURMD_NODENAME` / `hostname`) and the job id; the job must run on a CPU partition. Differences, if any,
most plausibly come from library/BLAS versions; report the outcome as measured either way.

**Paper conventions.** One table (per-method mean accuracy and 95% interval, paired difference and its interval,
interval method named, run comparison) and one figure (e.g. per-fold accuracies or paired differences across the
50 folds, or the selected-k distribution) must carry all claims. Every number in the text comes from code output in
`report/`.

